1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.200
To set the scene, let's take

2
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.700
a look at some common
failure modes of LLM-powered applications.

3
00:00:06.933 --> 00:00:09.700
Specifically, a RAG-power chatbot.

4
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:12.833
You'll explore these in the context
of a customer service chatbot

5
00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:16.600
for a small pizzeria, which is what
we have set up for you in the notebook.

6
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.100
Let's get started.

7
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.500
If you look at the current state
of the world for AI development, it's

8
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.700
very, very easy now with the set of tools
and libraries and frameworks

9
00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.900
that we have, to set up a proof of concept
of a generative AI application, right?

10
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:32.700
So your first RAG application

11
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.100
or your first agent workflows
are now really, really easy to build up.

12
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:41.333
But instead, what ends up taking a lot of
time as a developer is taking that POC

13
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.100
and getting it into a state
that is production ready.

14
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.000
There's a lot of reasons for this,
but the primary one

15
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.833
is that AI reliability is a novel problem

16
00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:55.700
that is very much a key blocker
to getting genai apps into production.

17
00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:00.900
So what reliability really means is that
you are working with foundation models,

18
00:01:01.233 --> 00:01:04.900
and these models can, you know, do
anything that you give them moderately

19
00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:07.733
well, right.
Building an AI application with it.

20
00:01:07.733 --> 00:01:11.533
You want that application to do one thing
and do it perfectly right.

21
00:01:11.533 --> 00:01:15.100
It's very important that this application,
you know, has very low failure rate.

22
00:01:16.033 --> 00:01:19.133
AI reliability ends up being a huge issue
for these reasons.

23
00:01:19.133 --> 00:01:20.500
And we look at this issue of AI

24
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:23.500
reliability from the point of view of RAG
applications.

25
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:27.400
You might already be familiar with RAG,
if you aren't,

26
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:31.433
we have a very high level
overview of what it looks like here.

27
00:01:31.433 --> 00:01:33.900
The fundamental idea is
you have a set of documents

28
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:36.700
that you'll kind of chunk up
and store into a vector database.

29
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.900
So this is your first set of inputs.

30
00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.833
The second set of input is a question

31
00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:44.233
that your end user
comes up with to your application.

32
00:01:44.733 --> 00:01:47.500
And then once you get a question
you basically like look at

33
00:01:47.500 --> 00:01:49.900
what is the document
that's more similar to the question

34
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:52.900
or the chunks that are more similar
to the question in your vector database.

35
00:01:52.933 --> 00:01:56.500
Retrieve those, combine the question
with your retrieved documents,

36
00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:59.800
and then send them over to an LLM
to get an answer.

37
00:01:59.833 --> 00:02:02.700
Right. So there's a very,
very high-level overview.

38
00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.733
And now let's pop over into our Jupyter
notebook

39
00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:09.500
and look at that RAG
workflow happening in parallel.

40
00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:14.800
So before we get started, let's just copy
over some code that helps us

41
00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:20.000
set up our warnings so that we have
a very clean notebook cell experience.

42
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.300
And then let's also import
some of our statements here.

43
00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:25.100
So we're going to be using OpenAI.

44
00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.600
So we've imported the OpenAI client.

45
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.333
And then we also have implemented
some quick and easy helper functions.

46
00:02:31.933 --> 00:02:34.233
The first is this RAG chat widget.

47
00:02:34.233 --> 00:02:37.833
And then the second is a very simple
in-memory vector data database.

48
00:02:38.033 --> 00:02:40.400
If you want to look at
how either of these is implemented,

49
00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.400
you can go into, you know, the helper
function which should be available to you.

50
00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:44.100
Awesome.

51
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.300
And now that we have these imported,

52
00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:50.000
let's build out our simple RAG
application here.

53
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.700
So the first thing we're going to do
is copy over a system message

54
00:02:55.033 --> 00:02:57.700
that you will need for your LLM.

55
00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:02.100
So I recommend taking a second to pause
and read through the system message.

56
00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:05.033
You know, like all system messages,
there's a lot of criteria here.

57
00:03:05.033 --> 00:03:09.033
But this gives you an idea of what we're
building, which is the customer support

58
00:03:09.033 --> 00:03:13.100
bot for a pizza shop,
which we are calling Alfredo's Pizza Cafe.

59
00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:15.000
And you're going to be able
to, you know, like

60
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.133
let your customers
do a few different things.

61
00:03:17.133 --> 00:03:20.000
So you want them to be able to,
you know, ask questions about the menu,

62
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.400
ask questions about delivery, offers,
maybe change passwords

63
00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:27.000
or account
details on the Alfredo Pizza Cafe website.

64
00:03:27.533 --> 00:03:30.433
And then you also have
some behavior instruction here like,

65
00:03:30.433 --> 00:03:32.433
you know,
do not discuss other pizza chains.

66
00:03:32.433 --> 00:03:36.600
Don't answer questions about topics
unrelated to Pizza Cafe, and also

67
00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:40.300
don't make up information if you know
enough information isn't provided, right?

68
00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:45.200
So this is a pretty realistic setting
of what the system prompt for a RAG

69
00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:48.000
application will look like,
and how you'll try to guide

70
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.633
the behavior of your chatbot.

71
00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:51.700
Awesome.

72
00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:55.900
Now that we have our system prompts
set up, let's set up a few more components

73
00:03:55.900 --> 00:03:59.433
we need for our RAG applications.

74
00:03:59.500 --> 00:04:02.500
I'm going to scroll this up here
so that we have this at the top.

75
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:07.000
And then let's now add our client.

76
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:11.900
So this is the main LLM building block
which will power our LLM application.

77
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.400
And we are going to use OpenAI for that.

78
00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:19.133
And then the third thing we need
is this vector database.

79
00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:22.400
So once again this is a very very simple
in-memory vector database.

80
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:26.233
There's this shared data drive
which I encourage all of you to like.

81
00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:29.800
Take a quick pause here
and explore that and see what's in there.

82
00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:33.800
You've created
a bunch of dummy documentation

83
00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:38.333
as if you were running a real pizzeria,
and it contains information about

84
00:04:38.333 --> 00:04:42.600
who is part of the company, the menu
items, the directions to get to the shop,

85
00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:46.400
what methods of payment you can accept,
and then you know what offers we have

86
00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:48.400
going on a bunch of other things, right?

87
00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.300
So that all of your customers
can come on to this chatbot

88
00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:54.833
and ask a very,
very diverse set of questions.

89
00:04:54.833 --> 00:04:57.733
So now we have all of the components
that we need for

90
00:04:57.733 --> 00:05:01.500
our RAG chat application which is system
message client and vector database.

91
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:04.800
Now let us use our helper

92
00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:08.700
function and initialize
the simple RAG chat application

93
00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.700
using the client, the system message,
and the vector database.

94
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.300
So I am going to do a quick test

95
00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:19.233
to see if I can ask a question
